    "objid": "0000",
    "raw": "Goal: attach programmatic SENSORS to the WorkItem loop -- each measuring one property of\nthe generated code -- and take a reading at every step (every work-item-done), not as a\none-time or periodic batch pass. A sensor is pure measurement: cheap, automatic,\ndeterministic where possible, and report-only (SOFT gate). Actuation -- thresholds,\nhard-fail vs soft-fail, which sensors gate what -- is policy and lives in frequentcommits\nor a future skill, per todo.py's mechanism-only doctrine. todo.py computes, stores, and\nreports readings; it never blocks on them.\n\nHost language is Python (todo.py). Target languages for v1: Python and Go. Everything\nbelow is chosen to be pip-installable or dependency-free from a Python host -- no Go\ntoolchain, npm, or JVM required to take a reading.\n\nSENSOR INVENTORY\n\n1. Complexity (per function, on functions touched by the step's diff)\n   - Cyclomatic complexity (McCabe): the v1 baseline. One engine covers both targets:\n     lizard (pip install lizard; pure Python; importable -- lizard.analyze_file() -- not\n     just a CLI). Supports Python, Go, and ~18 other languages, and reports CCN, NLOC,\n     function length, and parameter count per function in one pass.\n   - Python-only enrichment, same host process: radon (pip; importable) adds\n     Maintainability Index and Halstead metrics; the cognitive_complexity package adds\n     SonarSource-style cognitive complexity (nesting-weighted, closer to \"hard to hold\n     in your head\" than raw McCabe).\n   - Note: xenon (radon's threshold-failing wrapper) is a GATE, not a sensor -- exactly\n     the actuation this ticket keeps out of mechanism. Use radon in-process instead.\n   - Go cognitive complexity / MI: no pure-Python source today. Defer (options later:\n     tree-sitter-go queries, or accepting a Go-binary optional dependency).\n\n2. Size and shape proxies (free with the same lizard pass)\n   - Function length, nesting depth, parameter count. Weak signals alone, useful in\n     combination and for trend lines.\n\n3. Churn (git-derived; zero new dependencies)\n   - Reading: `git log --numstat --follow -- <path>` via the existing run_git plumbing\n     gives per-commit added/removed line counts; `git log -L` gives function-level churn\n     but is expensive -- reserve it for drill-down, not the per-step reading.\n   - Caveat (why churn must never be read alone): some churn is expected and healthy --\n     argument parsing grows with every new feature; this repo's configure_parser methods\n     change on every new flag. Bare churn counts false-positive on exactly that.\n   - Read it as complexity x churn (the CodeScene \"hotspot\" model):\n     high churn + high complexity = hotspot, the highest-value refactor target;\n     high churn + low complexity = growing surface area, likely fine;\n     low churn + high complexity = stable but risky, lower priority.\n     This combination -- not any single number -- is the honest answer to \"is\n     refactoring in order?\".\n   - pydriller (pip) exists if richer git mining is ever wanted; raw git suffices for v1.\n\n4. Change coupling / shotgun surgery (git-derived; zero new dependencies)\n   - The smell: a small, logically simple change that forces edits scattered across many\n     files usually means poor factoring (tight coupling, low cohesion) even when no\n     single touched function is individually complex. Complexity says \"this function is\n     hard to follow\"; this says \"this one intent required touching everywhere\".\n   - Reading, historical: which files change together in the same commit over a rolling\n     window -- `git log --name-only` grouped by commit; plain code, no tool needed.\n   - Reading, per-step: files touched in this WorkItem's diff vs the stated intent in\n     its summary (\"rename a field\" touching 15 files is a flag; \"add a subcommand\"\n     touching parser + tests + docs + skill is the expected shape).\n   - Caveat: needs a baseline of expected fan-out per change type, so the signal is\n     fan-out ABOVE the expected shape for that kind of change, not raw file count.\n\n5. Duplication / clone detection -- DEFERRED. The usual tools are wrong-host (jscpd is\n   npm, pmd-cpd is JVM); pylint's R0801 is Python-only and coarse. A token-based\n   detector over tree-sitter tokens is feasible later if the itch is real.\n\n6. Narrow LLM probe (the one non-deterministic sensor)\n   - A lesser (cheaper/faster) model, given PRECISE and narrow instructions, as a\n     targeted early reviewer per step: one well-specified question per pass -- \"does\n     this diff match its stated WorkItem summary?\", \"did an obvious edge case get\n     skipped?\", one named smell checked in isolation. A small model asked one narrow\n     question is far more reliable than the same model asked to \"review for quality\",\n     and cheap enough to run on every work-item-done.\n   - EARLY and ADDITIONAL, never a substitute: the final review by a capable model (or\n     human) before work is considered done stays. This probe just catches cheap, narrow\n     issues sooner so the expensive review is not the first line of defense.\n   - Which model: DEFERRED on purpose. Requirements are only (a) cheap enough per-step,\n     (b) follows narrow instructions reliably. Hide it behind the same sensor interface\n     (diff + question in, structured verdict out) so the model name is a config value,\n     not an architecture decision.\n\nREADING SHAPE (leaning, to be finalized when worked)\n\nScope each reading to the step: the diff prev-work-item-sha..HEAD (BaseSha for the first\nitem), computing per-function readings for touched functions plus a whole-file reading\nfor context. Attach readings to the `code` work item (e.g. a `sensors` object keyed by\nsensor name) so the WorkItems trail stays the self-describing ledger; give doctor an\ninformational tier BELOW its existing soft warnings (readings never fail doctor); roll a\nsummary into ActualSummary at finish for the human/capable-model review to read.\n\nAlso evaluate: the scan-repo skill already wraps a local CLI code scanner reporting\nmetrics/complexity/smells -- check whether it can serve as (or share) the sensor engine\nbefore building fresh.\n\nDEFERRED (explicitly not decided in this ticket)\n- Which lesser model powers the LLM probe, and its prompt library.\n- Thresholds and baselines (policy -- frequentcommits/skill layer), including the\n  expected-fan-out baseline for the coupling sensor.\n- Where sensors run relative to CI (per-step local is v1; CI re-run vs trusting\n  recorded readings is later).\n- Targets beyond Python and Go (JS/TS, Swift, shell); SonarQube-class platforms are out\n  of scope for v1 -- too heavy for a per-step sensor.\n- Duplication sensor (see above).\n- Go cognitive complexity / MI source."
  },
